; The eight queens claim, re-asked as a derivation (extension §2).
;
; This file exists to be CHECKED AGAINST queens.claims, not to replace it:
; `pol check` answers `solvable` with the checker's own reading, and
; `modality-cross-check.sh` re-asks the same question of the rules engine. Two
; implementations of one question; a disagreement is a bug in one of them.
;
; POLARITY. `solvable` is a `possible`, so it is encoded POSITIVELY: the
; relation holds the reachable situations that satisfy F, and NON-EMPTY means
; the property holds. (The counterexample-set encoding river.rules explains is
; for `live`, and nothing here is one.)

(load "stdlib.pol")

; Reachability over real edges, reflexive at every situation — the same graph
; the checker walks. Cheap here for a reason worth noting: queens.pol is the
; COLUMN-ORDERED model, so its graph is a tree of 2057 situations with 2056
; edges and no cycles. The unordered variant beside it has 118969 situations,
; and this transitive closure over that would be its own performance essay.
(relation reach 2)
(rule (reach S S) (situation S))
(rule (reach S T) (edge E S M) (reach M T))

; `possible`: the boards, reachable from the empty one, with every queen
; placed. Non-empty ⟺ `holds solvable`.
;
; The guard is the same one queens.claims asks, `all` and all — G is a guard
; DATUM, and forms expand in a .rules file exactly as they do in a model, so
; this is the stdlib `all` reaching the engine already rewritten into the
; kernel's `some`.
(relation solvable 1)
(rule (solvable T)
  (init S)
  (reach S T)
  (holds T (all (q queen) (defined q.row))))

; The per-queen view the conjunction above cannot give: where each queen stands
; in a given situation. `pol derive queens.pol queens.rules "(placed 2056 Q R)"`
; reads a finished board off the space.
;
; The typed declaration is not decoration. `R` occurs only here and in a
; sort-transparent position, so nothing else in the rule could seed it — and a
; row is what the answer is about.
(relation placed (Situation queen row-t))
(rule (placed S Q R) (situation S) (holds S (is Q.row R)))
